\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Collision and relaxation timescales in a mass versus radius diagram. Dashed lines correspond to the critical mass $M_{\rm crit}$, while solid lines correspond to the relaxation mass $M_{\rm relax}$, both calculated for solar-mass clusters. The colours represent two different characteristic timescales: blue for $t_{\rm H}=5 \times 10^6~{\rm yr}$, and red for $t_{\rm H}=10^{10}~{\rm yr}$. The black dash-dotted line represents $t_{\rm coll} = t_{\rm relax}$, with $t_{\rm H}$ varying from $10^2$ yr to $10^{10}$ yr. Clusters above the black dash-dotted line satisfy $t_{\rm coll} \leq t_{\rm relax}$. For each color, the collisional regime (CR) is defined by $t_{\rm coll} \leq t_{\rm H} \leq t_{\rm relax}$ (green colored zone); the stable regime is where $t_{\rm relax} \leq t_{\rm H} \leq t_{\rm coll}$; and the intermediate regime (IR) fulfils $t_{\rm relax} \leq t_{\rm coll} \leq t_{\rm H}$.}}{9}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Principal domains in AMUSE framework. Multiple physical problems can be solved coupling codes from different domains. Credits: \citet {zwart2018astrophysical}.}}{12}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the three-layer architecture of the AMUSE framework. The user interacts with the system via a Python script (top layer), which communicates through interfaces (middle layer) with the community codes (bottom layer). Credits: \citet {zwart2018astrophysical}.}}{13}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Representation of an oct tree}}{20}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Comparison of force calculation normalized (masses and gravitational constant set to 1). In dashed green, the exact gravitational force. In blue, the force with plummer softening, and in red, the force calculated with cubic spline softening.}}{28}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Scheme of the \texttt {BRIDGE} coupling strategy. In the kick steps, \texttt {FASTKICK} computes the mutual gravitational forces between stars and gas, and the velocities of both components are updated. In the drift step, each code evolves its particles independently: \texttt {Ph4} handles stellar dynamics (including collision detection and mergers), while \texttt {Fi} evolves the gas. After the kick-drift-kick cycle, the global \texttt {BRIDGE} time step is updated based on the gas free-fall time and the coupling timescales ($t_{\rm ff}$, $\Delta t_{\rm coup, gas}$, $\Delta t_{\rm coup, stars}$). This updated timestep is used for both the next \texttt {BRIDGE} step and the \texttt {Fi} integration. Additionally, escaped N-body particles are identified and tagged in the post-processing phase.}}{30}{figure.caption.18}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Energy conservation and virial equilibrium for a test simulation of a gas-embedded star cluster. Top panel: relative energy error $\Delta E / E$. Bottom panel: virial ratio $2K/|U|$. The system remains in virial equilibrium with an energy error of $\lesssim 10^{-4}$ over 50 crossing times.}}{44}{figure.caption.28}%
